# Security Policy — {{PROJECT}}

> **What this file is for, so it does not get filled with the wrong thing.**
>
> `SECURITY.md` is a **public contact channel**: it tells someone who finds a vulnerability how to
> report it privately instead of opening a public issue. GitHub surfaces it in the Security tab and
> links it from the "Report a vulnerability" flow.
>
> It is **not** where your security rules for development live. Those belong in:
> - `AGENTS.md` → `{{SENSITIVE_DATA}}`, `{{RED_ZONE}}`, `{{APPROVED_VENDORS}}`
> - `.claude/skills/compliance/` → the gate that runs before code
> - `.claude/skills/complete-security-review/` → the audit procedure
>
> **Worth having when** the repo is public, or private but with contributors outside your team.
> **Skip it** on a solo private repo — an unread file that promises a response time you do not honour
> is worse than no file. Delete this whole comment block before publishing.

## Reporting a vulnerability

**Do not open a public issue for a security problem.** A public report is a disclosure, and it
reaches attackers at the same time it reaches us.

Report privately via:

- **GitHub Security Advisories** — the "Report a vulnerability" button in this repository's Security
  tab (preferred: it gives us a private thread with you)
- **Email:** `<security@yourdomain>`

Please include, as far as you can:

1. what the issue is and where (file, endpoint, or URL);
2. steps to reproduce, or a proof of concept;
3. what an attacker gains — data read, data written, access escalated;
4. who can trigger it: anyone, any authenticated user, or only a privileged account.

## What to expect

| Stage | Target |
|---|---|
| Acknowledgement | within `{{INCIDENT_DEADLINE}}` |
| Initial assessment | <X business days> |
| Fix or mitigation plan | <depends on severity — state your real target> |

⚠️ Put times here you will actually meet. A missed promise in this file is read as neglect, and it is
the first thing a reporter quotes when they go public.

We will keep you updated, and we will tell you when the issue is fixed. If you would like credit in
the advisory, say so and we will name you.

## Scope

**In scope:** <the application, its API, its infrastructure — be specific>

**Out of scope:**

- findings from automated scanners with no demonstrated impact
- social engineering of the team, or physical attacks
- vulnerabilities in third-party services we do not control
- <anything else that genuinely does not apply>

## Safe harbour

We will not pursue or support legal action against anyone who reports a vulnerability in good faith,
follows this policy, avoids privacy violations and service disruption, and gives us reasonable time
to fix the issue before disclosing it publicly.

